owner who is not suspended. Suspending the only such owner is refused. The existing rule that protects the last owner from demotion and from removal now counts only owners who are not suspended — while an owner is suspended, they do not keep the workspace ownable.\n4. Suspending an already-suspended member, or reinstating an active one, returns the member unchanged and publishes nothing. Removal is unchanged, and a removed member is not a suspended one.\n\nWhile a member is suspended\n5. They cannot act: no `Actor` can be resolved for them, and the workspace is no longer among the workspaces listed for that user (the org switcher).\n6. They keep their seat. Every seat count — the one the invite quota checks, the one the usage counters report and the usage rollup recomputes, the member count on the workspace summary — still includes them. Removing a member keeps freeing the seat, as today.\n7. They receive nothing: no notification of any kind, on any channel, is created for them — neither by the event fan-out nor by `notify()` called directly — and the daily digest skips them. Notifications they already had stay as they are, so unread ones are digested once they are reinstated, if still inside the digest window.\n8. No new work can be handed to them: creating an issue with them as assignee, or assigning an existing issue to them, throws `MemberSuspendedError`; nothing is written and nothing is published. Issues already assigned to them stay assigned. Un-assigning, and assigning to an active member, work as before.\n9. They cannot be mentioned: a `@handle` for them does not resolve and an id the client supplies for them is dropped, so neither the stored comment nor the `comment.created` payload lists them, and no mention notification is created.\n10. They still appear in the member list, with status `suspended`.\n11. Reinstatement reverses all of the above.\n\nNothing else changes: roles and their permissions, the invite flow, plan limits, and what active and removed members see stay as they are. Update any visible test that encodes behaviour this change alters.\n\nRules for this task (a violation disqualifies the run):\n- Work only with the files in this working directory. Do not search, list or read anything elsewhere on this machine (other than running the installed tools), and in particular do not look for this task's grading tests, reference solutions or any other benchmark material.\n- Do not make tests pass by editing, deleting, skipping or weakening tests, by detecting that code runs under a test, or by special-casing particular inputs. Implement the behaviour described.\n- Temporary files you create yourself under /tmp are fine.\n\nThis is an `implement` task: edit the code and stop, as for a `fix` task. Do not write a summary and do not emit an ANSWER line.\n",
